Subject: [PATCH] minimum arm64: install R Chromium right after a real install

A fresh install had no browser on it until someone opened R AppMarket (or
ran pkgman by hand) to fetch R Chromium themselves. Since the RENKU Apps
repository config is already on the image (0008) and R AppMarket proves
the repository itself is reachable from first boot (0020), there is no
reason to wait for a person to do that by hand.

Adds a new `renku-install-rchromium` job to data/launch/user's `desktop`
target, modeled directly on the existing `create-installer-link` and
`update-time` jobs already there:

- Gated on `not read_only`, the same condition `create-installer-link`
  uses to tell a real install apart from the live/install medium itself
  -- the live medium's own write area is far too small for a browser
  package (this is the exact "No space left on device" pkgman error a
  300 MiB live medium gives for this install), so the job must never run
  there.
- Triggered `on network_available`, the same condition `update-time`
  already uses to wait for the network stack, since pkgman needs it.
- Gated on a marker file the job's own script
  (data/system/boot/renku-install-rchromium) writes, but only once
  `pkgman install -y rchromium` actually succeeds -- a boot with no
  network yet or an unreachable server leaves the marker unwritten, so
  the job simply tries again on the next boot instead of a failed first
  attempt losing the one-shot marker for good.

This deliberately does not reuse the stock `first-login`/PostInstallScript
mechanism (`~/config/settings/first_login` + `/boot/system/boot/first-login/
*.sh`): that marker is deleted unconditionally once the job runs, success
or not, so a script dropped in there gets exactly one try, ever, with no
way to retry a transient failure.

diff --git a/build/jam/images/definitions/minimum b/build/jam/images/definitions/minimum
index 7d742c66..9c6fd187 100644
--- a/build/jam/images/definitions/minimum
+++ b/build/jam/images/definitions/minimum
@@ -490,3 +490,12 @@ if $(HAIKU_PACKAGING_ARCHS[1]) = arm64 {
 	AddFilesToHaikuImage system settings package-repositories
 		: $(renkuApps) : "RENKU Apps" ;
 }
+
+# On arm64, install R Chromium from that same repository right after a
+# real install (data/launch/user's renku-install-rchromium job runs this).
+if $(HAIKU_PACKAGING_ARCHS[1]) = arm64 {
+	local renkuInstallRChromium = renku-install-rchromium ;
+	SEARCH on $(renkuInstallRChromium)
+		= [ FDirName $(HAIKU_TOP) data system boot ] ;
+	AddFilesToHaikuImage system boot : $(renkuInstallRChromium) ;
+}
diff --git a/data/launch/user b/data/launch/user
index b0ef5602..7cef7f2c 100644
--- a/data/launch/user
+++ b/data/launch/user
@@ -52,6 +52,21 @@ target desktop {
 		requires x-vnd.Be-TSKB
 	}

+	job renku-install-rchromium {
+		# Only on a real install (never the live/install medium itself,
+		# where the write area is far too small for a browser package),
+		# and only once: the script writes the marker itself, but only
+		# on a successful "pkgman install", so a boot with no network
+		# yet or an unreachable server retries on the next boot instead
+		# of losing the one-shot marker for good.
+		if {
+			not read_only
+			not file_exists ~/config/settings/renku-rchromium-installed
+		}
+		launch /bin/sh /boot/system/boot/renku-install-rchromium
+		on network_available
+	}
+
 	job create-installer-link {
 		# When run from a read-only medium a.k.a. live desktop
 		if {
diff --git a/data/system/boot/renku-install-rchromium b/data/system/boot/renku-install-rchromium
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..eec98f42
--- /dev/null
+++ b/data/system/boot/renku-install-rchromium
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+#!/bin/sh
+# Installs R Chromium from the RENKU Apps repository on the first boot of a
+# real install (see data/launch/user's renku-install-rchromium job: gated on
+# "not read_only", so this never runs on the live/install medium itself).
+# The marker is only written on success, so a failed attempt (no network
+# yet, server unreachable) retries on the next boot instead of giving up.
+
+marker=~/config/settings/renku-rchromium-installed
+
+pkgman refresh >/var/log/renku-rchromium-install.log 2>&1
+if pkgman install -y rchromium >>/var/log/renku-rchromium-install.log 2>&1; then
+	touch "$marker"
+fi
